func=jag::game::ServerConnection::ReadIncomingMessage	func_addr=0x00100010	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=959	cu_index_from_entry=216	text=HOOK-CRITICAL: BUF_POS (conn+0x2d8) is RESET TO ZERO here, immediately after the payload read and\njust before PAYLOAD_READY (+0x35) is set. The fast-path re-entry at 0x000ff4d4 zeroes it too.\n\nSo on return from ReadIncomingMessage the cursor is 0, NOT the payload length. A capture hook that\nuses BUF_POS as the byte count reads zero bytes. Use RESOLVED_SIZE (conn+0x30) as the length.\n\nAlso note the payload is read to the START of the buffer (the Read dst is conn+0x2d0 itself, not\n+pos), so the packet body is bufData[0 .. resolvedSize).
func=jag::game::ServerConnection::ReadIncomingMessage	func_addr=0x00100010	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=1008	cu_index_from_entry=228	text=FAILURE / INCOMPLETE-PACKET EXIT. Every short-read path lands here and writes BOTH halves of the\nout shared_ptr as NULL, then returns the out pointer in RAX.\n\nReached from: stream null or stream state !\e 2; GetAvailable()\e\e0 before the opcode byte;\nGetAvailable()<1 before the g1 size; GetAvailable()<2 before the g2 size; GetAvailable()<resolvedSize\nbefore the payload; and the unknown-opcode path at 0x000ff494.\nThe 0x000ff627 path (opcode valid, no ProtEntry) stores the global at 0x00ecd350/0x00ecd358, which\nis {0,0} - also an empty shared_ptr.\n\n\e> outMessage[1] \e\e NULL is a COMPLETE and reliable "no packet this call" discriminator. It is\nexactly what the caller FUN_000e9540 tests. A capture hook must gate on it or it will log torn\nbuffers as real packets.
func=jag::game::ServerConnection::ReadIncomingMessage	func_addr=0x00100010	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=1316	cu_index_from_entry=309	text=HOOK-CRITICAL: the returned TcpConnectionMessage's OWN buffer receives only ONE byte - the prot\nopcode (msg+0x18 data, msg+0x20 pos incremented to 1). The PAYLOAD never enters the message; it\nstays in the ServerConnection's Packet at conn+0x2d0.\n\nConfirmed by the consumer FUN_000e9540, which hands the handler conn+0x2c0 (the ServerConnection's\nPacket), not the message's buffer, and takes only the prot from msg+0x38 and the size from msg+0x4.\n\nA hook that reads the returned message's buffer captures a single opcode byte and no body.
func=jag::game::HeightMap::SampleTileLinkHeightOffset	func_addr=0x003bed20	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::game::HeightMap::SampleTileLinkHeightOffset (Linux twin ). Additive per-vertex location/bridge height offset. mode 1 \e> int16 s0; mode 2 \e> max(s0,s1); else 0. col/row clamped [0,63].\nProves ORegionHeightContainer: LINK_HEIGHT_PLANE_COUNT\e0x1d0 (0x3c48ef), LINK_HEIGHT_GRID\e0x1d8 (0x3c4935), LINK_PLANE_STRIDE\e0x4000 (SHL 6  + SHL 6  + *4 ), LINK_COL_STRIDE\e0x100 (0x3c4921/4924/493c), LINK_ROW_STRIDE\e0x4 (0x3c493c). All identical to Linux.
func=jag::input::DispatchLowLevelMouseMessage	func_addr=0x0072e830	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::input::DispatchLowLevelMouseMessage(hookState, message, MSLLHOOKSTRUCT *)\n\nBody of the WH_MOUSE_LL hook. param_3 is an MSLLHOOKSTRUCT, so param_3[1].y is its `flags` field --\nthis is where MSLLHOOKSTRUCT.flags (bit 0 \e LLMHF_INJECTED) enters the client.\n\nFilters first: returns immediately for 0x2a0/0x2a2 and the 0xa0..0xad WM_NC* range; converts the screen\npoint to client coordinates via GetClientRect + ClientToScreen and drops anything outside the client\nrect or not over the game window (WindowFromPoint).\n\nThen, for each InputHandler in the red-black tree at (hookState+0xf8 -> vtable+0x50) plus the single\nhandler at that container+0x8:\n  message 0x200 (WM_MOUSEMOVE)                       -> DispatchMouseMoveWithSource\n  messages 0x201 0x203 0x204 0x206 0x207 0x209       -> DispatchMouseEventWithSource\n  anything else                                      -> not dispatched at all\nNote the absence of the *BUTTONUP messages (0x202/0x205/0x208): a physical press produces exactly one\nnative button sample, and a double-click produces two (DOWN then DBLCLK).
func=jag::game::PathingEntity::UpdateHighlight	func_addr=0x00130420	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Windows counterpart of Linux 949-4 jag::game::PathingEntity::UpdateHighlight  - identical\nskeleton: same entity+0x50 guard, same [[entity+0x50]+0x20]+0xb8/0xb9/0xba byte gate, same 16-byte-stride\nhighlight category table (&DAT_00e9e790, 8 entries, case 8 special), same combat-target branch.\nProves the whole ORenderModel highlight block is IDENTICAL to Linux:\n  renderModel \e [entity+0xc68]   (OEntity.RENDER_MODEL; Linux 0xc58)\n  +0x100 HL_COLOR_R   +0x104 HL_COLOR_G   +0x108 HL_COLOR_B   +0x10c HL_BASE_ALPHA (float, gate `<\e 0`)\n  +0x110 HL_STRENGTH  +0x114 HL_FACTOR    +0x134 HL_MODE (byte)\nCombat-target branch also cross-confirms OLoggedInPlayer.PLAYER_INDEX\e0x48 and\nOPathingEntity.SERVER_INDEX\e0x88: [[[entity+0x1078]+0x19f68]+0x48] \e\e [entity+0x88].
func=jag::game::AnimationWrapper::EmitFrameEvents	func_addr=0x002f79f0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Walks the frames played since the last tick and fires the wrapper's per-frame event callback for each. [this+0x24] is the frame cursor the engine exposes as OAnimation.CURRENT_FRAME; it is compared against the playback state's counter at [[this+0x18]+0x98] and written back on every return path. gcc inlines this whole loop into AnimationWrapper::Update.
func=jag::hal::JagRenderViewProc	func_addr=0x0072dc60	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=WndProc for window class "JagRenderView" (registered by jag::hal::RegisterJagRenderViewClass\n, lpfnWndProc at WNDCLASSEXA+0x08 - NOT +0x10).\nTHIS IS THE WINDOWS INPUT ENTRY POINT. The client uses TWO window classes:\n  "JagWindow"     -> jag::hal::JagWindowProc  - top-level frame. Handles ONLY window\n                     management (WM_NCCREATE 0x81, WM_DESTROY 3, WM_SIZE 5, WM_SETFOCUS 7,\n                     WM_CLOSE 0x10, WM_GETMINMAXINFO 0x24, WM_COMMAND 0x111,\n                     WM_ENTERSIZEMOVE/EXITSIZEMOVE 0x231/0x232). NO keyboard or mouse messages.\n                     It forwards focus and sizing to the child HWND at frame[0xe0]->[0x20].\n  "JagRenderView" -> this - the child render/input window; receives all WM_KEY*/WM_CHAR/WM_MOUSE*.\nThis function only stashes the owner at GWLP_USERDATA on WM_NCCREATE, registers device\nnotification (DBT_DEVTYP_DEVICEINTERFACE, 0x20-byte filter), then tail-calls the real handler\nFUN_00728830(owner, hwnd, msg, wParam, lParam) - which is where the WM_KEYDOWN/WM_MOUSEMOVE/\nWM_MOUSEWHEEL dispatch into jag::input::Input::On* lives. NOT YET ANALYSED.\nFrame-object fields seen here and in JagWindowProc: +0xe0 child-view wrapper (HWND at +0x20),\n+0xe8/+0xec client width/height as float, +0x148/+0x14c min size, +0x238 resize-dirty flag,\n+0x23c dpi scale numerator, +0x240 own HWND, +0x289 in-size-move flag.
func=jag::ProjectileList::Add	func_addr=0x00127860	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ProjectileList::Add - appends a projectile. this+0x8 \e jag::Client*.\nList fields: +0x10 head sentinel, +0x18 tail, +0x20/+0x28/+0x30/+0x38 node pool/freelist,\n+0x50 live count (hard cap 100 - the oldest is evicted first).\nPooled ProjectileEntity stride 0x1C0; construction is FUN_001a8d50 (the ProjectileEntity ctor,\nwhich calls jag::game::Entity::Entity). GraphNode+0x1A0 is set to the new entity (back-ptr).\nOClient.SCENE_MANAGER \e 0x19990 on Windows: client[0x19990] is the SceneManager, and\nGetActiveWorld is inlined here as `idx \e sm[0x70]; world \e sm[0x58] + idx*0x10` (shared_ptr array),\nbailing to the null-world singleton when idx \e\e -1.\nEvidence: (1) the `count \e\e 100` eviction guard and the identical intrusive-list/freelist field\nset; (2) the 0x1C0 pool element stride, identical to Linux; (3) the same 19-argument signature and\nthe same tail (push node, bump count, write the entity back-ptr into GraphNode+0x1A0).
