func=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct	func_addr=0x00147100	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MainLogicManager::Construct(this, client) -- allocated 0x7898 bytes, stored at client+0x198e0.\n\nAuthoritative proof of the two mouse rings' geometry. Two initialisation loops, both stride 0x28 and\nboth zeroing sample+0x04..+0x13 while seeding sample+0x20 \e 1 (the same field pattern as\nNativeMouseSample):\n  loop 1: 50 iterations from this+0x4700  -> entry base this+0x46f0, ends at this+0x4ec0\n          followed by an 8-byte zero store at this+0x4ec0, clearing write(+0x4ec0)/read(+0x4ec4)\n  loop 2: 200 iterations from this+0x4ed8 -> entry base this+0x4ec8, ends at this+0x6e08\n          followed by an 8-byte zero store at this+0x6e08, clearing write(+0x6e08)/read(+0x6e0c)\nSo: CLICK ring \e 50 x MouseSample at +0x46f0; MOVEMENT ring \e 200 x MouseSample at +0x4ec8. Adjacent,\nindependent, identical entry layout.\n\nAlso registers three listener objects into the client's InputHandler (client+0x18E50) via the\nlistener-set insert at handler+0x980, and two callables into its own listener vectors. None of those\nregistrations is the ring producer -- the object at this+0x7090 carries vtable 0x00b5a690 whose\nnon-destructor slots are all input-module base-class defaults (touch/pointer), not ring pushes.
func=jag::graphics::GraphNode::GetWorldTranslation	func_addr=0x00032b20	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Lazily recomputes and returns &amp;node-&gt;sceneTranslation. GraphNode layout confirmed here: +0xd0/+0xd4/+0xd8 local translation, +0xf0/+0xf4/+0xf8 accumulated scene translation, +0xfc ushort flags (bit 0x2 \e world transform dirty, bit 0x4 set after recompute, bit 0x9 \e bounds dirty, bit 0x200 \e has entity), +0x130 parent. Accumulation is child.local + parent.world component-wise. Raw component order is {X, height, depth}: ScenePicker::TestEntityHit squares components [0] and [2] against the camera X/Y at Client+0xe83b8/+0xe83c0 for a ground-plane distance, leaving [1] (+0xf4) as the vertical axis.
func=jag::ClientStream::Close	func_addr=0x00641f60	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ClientStream::Close(socket) - `this` here is the nested socket sub-object at ClientStream+0x8.\nClears the eastl::string at +0x20 (SSO marker byte at +0x27 restored to 0x17), zeroes the u16 at\n+0x48, sets the fd/SOCKET at +0x0 to -1 and calls ws2_32 closesocket (Ordinal_3).\nEvidence: identical field set and identical ordering to Linux jag::ClientStream::Close (which ends\nin close(fd) instead of closesocket), and it is the close used by Read/Write/Flush/GetAvailable.
func=jag::MiniMenu::MiniMenu	func_addr=0x00165610	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::MiniMenu constructor. Builds the per-family action arrays, then calls the only two DoAction\nregistration functions in the binary:\n  jag::MiniMenu::RegisterComponentDoActions   -  7 actions (interface-component family)\n  jag::MiniMenu::RegisterDoActions            - 59 actions\n\e> 66 DoActions total for this build. NOTE: the inline list in RegisterDoActions' own comment is\nlabelled "61 entries" but actually enumerates 59; 59 is correct, 61 is a miscount.\n\nThe arrays built here are what fix the canonical op ordering, independent of the registration order:\n  loc array          c6aae0 c6ab40 c6aba0 c6ac00 c6ac60 c6acc0 -> ids 2 3 4 5 1001 1002 \e OBJECT_1..6\n  ground-item array  c6b210 c6b260 c6b2b0 c6b300 c6b350 c6b3a0 -> ids 18 19 20 21 22 1004 \e GROUND_ITEM_1..6\n  player array       c6b3f0..c6b6c0 then c6b710..c6b9e0       -> ids 44..53 then 2044..2053\nThis is what proves OBJECT_5/OBJECT_6 are ids 1001/1002 and NOT the alias ids 6/7, which merely\nreuse the same senders.
func=jag::Client::Client	func_addr=0x0001cb40	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::Client::Client(Client*) - CERTAIN.\nSole constructor. Called from the app bootstrap FUN_0001b7d0 immediately after\noperator new(0x53818, 16); the result is stored into both jag::g_client (0x00e8e490) and\njag::g_app (0x00c883a8). Windows Client object size \e 0x53818 bytes.\nEvidence (3 independent):\n 1. Allocation-then-construct pair in the bootstrap, whose alternate --compileshader branch builds\n    a 0x18D20-byte sibling app from the same base - i.e. this is the game-app object.\n 2. Installs the Client vtable at +0x00 (PTR_FUN_00b4fe10) plus secondary vptrs at +0x508 and\n    +0x18D08, and stores the literal "client" (used later as the config section name in\n    Client::Init at 0x0001f9f0).\n 3. Spawns the Js5WorkerThread / MixerWorker / VorbisWorker worker threads - the client's own\n    worker set.\nFields initialised here that are load-bearing: mainState int @ +0x19F60 \e 0,\nconnection/manager pointer slots across +0x19818..+0x19A00 all zeroed (they are populated later by\nthe manager-construction pass FUN_000223c0), window size @ +0x537D8 \e 1280 x 720.\nBase sub-object occupies 0x00..0x18D20; Client-specific data starts at 0x18D08 (third vptr).
