func=jag::game::ServerConnection::FlushClientMessages	func_addr=0x002f47f0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=ServerConnection outgoing flush. bool Flush(ServerConnection* conn).\n\nDrains the pending-message intrusive list at conn+0x48/+0x50, and for each message calls\n  jag::ClientStream::Write(conn->stream /* +0x08 */, msg->bufData /* +0x18 */, msg->payloadLen /* +0x28 */)\nthen jag::ClientStream::Flush(conn->stream) once the queue empties.\n\nCONFIRMS (Windows-only evidence, no Linux import):\n  ServerConnection +0x08 \e ClientStream*  -- passed as `this` to Write/Flush here, and as `this`\n      to ClientStream::Read/GetAvailable in ReadIncomingMessage ; ProcessConnections\n       guards on **(int**)(conn+8) \e\e 2. Three independent functions.\n  TcpConnectionMessage +0x18 \e buffer data ptr (the `src` arg of Write)\n  TcpConnectionMessage +0x28 \e payload byte count (the `len` arg of Write)\n\nOther ServerConnection fields used here: +0x58 freelist head, +0x60/+0x68 pool bump/end,\n+0x70 node stride, +0x80 pool block base, +0x88 pending count, +0x2b0 pending bytes,\n+0x2e4 lifetime bytes-sent counter.\nHYPOTHESIS: no official symbol recovered - do not rename on this evidence alone.
func=jag::ClientProt::RecordNativeMouseMove	func_addr=0x00179830	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::ClientProt::RecordNativeMouseMove -- producer of the NATIVE movement ring, and the Win32-only\ncounterpart of the game-event producer\njag::MainLogicManager::MouseInputRecorder::OnMouseMove (see the Linux program, where that\ndelegate is named and fully documented).\n\nCADENCE: also once per OS mouse message, but on a DIFFERENT and LOWER-LEVEL path. It has exactly\none reference in the whole image, a DATA reference from a delegate/listener table, so it is\ninvoked as a bound callback, never called directly. Its argument list is the proof of which path:\nbesides x and y it receives the Win32 MESSAGE ID and a SOURCE-FLAGS word, neither of which the\ngame-event onMouseMove delegate has or could supply. So the two movement rings are fed by two\nindependent producers on two independent event streams; they are not two views of one path.\n\nIt early-returns false unless the connection is established, so nothing is recorded outside the\nin-game session. It writes the sample's button field to -1 (movement), x/y as floats, the global\nclock as the timestamp, then the message id and the source flags, and applies the same\ndrop-oldest wrap rule as the game ring. The source-flags word is what\njag::ClientProt::WriteNativeMouseMoveSourceByte later appends per history sample, one byte each.
