func=jag::WorldLobbyData::GetHTTPURL	func_addr=0x001639d0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Builds the "scheme://host:port" content URL for a world or lobby endpoint. Windows counterpart of the Linux jag::WorldLobbyData::GetHTTPURL.\n\nScheme and port selection: when the ClientParameters TLS flag is set it emits https:// with the explicitly configured secure port; otherwise it emits http:// and picks the live literal content port if the server profile is the live/default one, else derives the port by adding a per-server-type base constant to the world id.\n\nRegister use differs from the Linux build: the WorldLobbyData is the first integer argument and the caller-supplied string buffer is the second, rather than the sret-first order the Itanium ABI gives the Linux build.
func=jag::WorldLobbyData::GetHTTPURL	func_addr=0x001639d0	kind=plate	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=Setting prototype: eastl_fixed_string_256 * GetHTTPURL(WorldLobbyData * this, eastl_fixed_string_256 * result)
func=jag::WorldLobbyData::GetHTTPURL	func_addr=0x001639d0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=82	cu_index_from_entry=20	text=Project X patcher target: the live-mode HTTP content port literal. Used when the ClientParameters TLS flag is clear and the server profile is the live/default one; the runtime patcher rewrites this immediate with the port from PROJECTX_HTTP_PORT.\n\nHow the patcher locates it: by the pair of world-port base constants this same basic block adds to the world id, not by the surrounding opcode bytes. Those differ between compilers, between builds, and between the Linux build's two copies of this logic.\n\nSite count is platform-specific and must be re-checked every build. This MSVC build keeps GetHTTPURL out of line and reaches it only through direct calls, so this is its only occurrence; the Linux build of the same revision additionally inlines the whole body into jag::ClientCrashReportingDataProvider::GetAddress and needs both copies patched.
func=jag::game::NPC::NPC	func_addr=0x003187e0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=NPC ctor. Identified by its sole caller FUN_001af2a0, which allocates from a pool of stride 0x11b0, places the NPC at poolSlot+0x20, and passes entityType \e 1 - the value Entity+0x10 must hold for the NPC branch. PathingEntity's own range ends at 0x1078; every field this ctor writes from param_1[0x20f] (+0x1078) upward is NPC-specific, and the object ends around +0x1178. +0x1078 holds the type pointer passed in by the caller.\nDO NOT import the Linux ONPC offsets. ID 0x1060 / TYPE_ID 0x1064 are inside PathingEntity's range on Windows (the base ctor writes 0x10001 at +0x1060). The NPC block appears shifted by roughly +0x18 relative to Linux, but that is arithmetic, not evidence - none of ID, TYPE_ID, CURRENT_HP, MAX_HP, HIDDEN_MENUOP_FLAGS or SHOW_AS_IMPORTANT has a confirmed Windows offset. Closing them needs a semantic read: the health-bar path for the HP pair, and the highlight_set_npc_show_as_important CS2 opcode handler (which checks entityType \e\e 1 then writes a bool) for the rest.
