func=jag::opcode::cam_forceangle	func_addr=0x00095a00	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=CS2 opcode CAM_FORCEANGLE(int xAngle, int yAngle).\nPops 2 ints off the ClientScriptState int stack (base +0x100, sp +0x10a0, sp -\e 2):\n  slot[sp]   \e xAngle (pitch)   slot[sp+1] \e yAngle (yaw)\nGuards on Client+0x19f68 (LOGGED_IN_PLAYER) !\e 0, then loads the camera from\nClient+0x19858 (OClient.CAMERA) and tail-calls jag::game::Camera::ForceAngle(cam, pitch, yaw).\nReturns the CS2 success constant DAT_00c69670 on success, DAT_00c696e0 otherwise.\n\nEvidence:\n - Camera::ForceAngle has exactly two callers: this function and\n   jag::PacketHandlers::CamForceangle (the CAM_FORCEANGLE ServerProt handler), so the\n   opcode identity is unambiguous.\n - Camera::ForceAngle clamps arg1 into [0x80,0x180] and wraps arg2 modulo 0x800 \e>\n   arg1 is the pitch/x angle (RS3 camera pitch range) and arg2 the 0..2047 yaw/y angle.\n - cs2-dumps: CAM_FORCEANGLE(int,int) is the only 2-int camera-angle setter; call sites\n   pass CAM_GETANGLE_XA() first and a 0..2047 value second, e.g. CAM_FORCEANGLE(268, 644).\n - Linux 949-5 jag::PacketHandlers::CamForceangle has the identical downstream shape.
