func=jag::opcode::highlight_set_npc_show_as_important	func_addr=0x001c14b0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::opcode::highlight_set_npc_show_as_important - CS2 opcode handler.\nIdentified by its OWN string literal "highlight_set_npc_show_as_important" (0xb5f820, sole xref).\n\nGates on Entity+0x10 \e\e 1 (entityType NPC - the value NPC::NPC  passes), pops one int\noff the CS2 int stack and stores (v > 0) as a single bool.\n\n\e> ONPC.SHOW_AS_IMPORTANT \e 0x1120 on Windows. CERTAIN, two independent lines:\n   (1) the single bool this handler writes, on a pointer proven to be an NPC by the type gate;\n   (2) jag::game::NPC::NPC  zeroes exactly one byte at +0x1120 (param_1[0x224]).\n\nNOTE FOR FUTURE PORTS: the Linux value is 0x1100 and an earlier pass hypothesised a +0x18 block\nshift (which would have given 0x1118). The real shift is +0x20. The refusal to record the\narithmetic guess was correct - it would have been wrong by 8 bytes and read a neighbouring field.
func=jag::graphics::GraphNode::GraphNode	func_addr=0x004c81d0	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::graphics::GraphNode::GraphNode (Windows). Field-for-field identical to the Linux 949-4 ctor\n at EVERY offset - the whole GraphNode layout can be ported 1:1:\n  0x00..0x3f  zeroed 4x4 world transform matrix (GetWorldTransformationMatrixAligned  writes\n              its rows at 0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30 and the translation at 0x30/0x34/0x38 with 1.0f at 0x3c)\n              \e> OGraphNode.DIRECTION_X/Y/Z \e 0x0/0x8/0x10 are matrix elements m00/m02/m10\n  0x40/0x44/0x48 \e +FLT_MAX, 0x4c pad ; 0x50/0x54/0x58 \e -FLT_MAX, 0x5c \e -1.0f   (transformed AABB)\n  0x60..0x7f     same sentinels                                                    (source AABB)\n  0x80..0xbf     world matrix scratch ; 0xd0/0xd4/0xd8 local pos (0xdc \e 4th slot of that qword pair)\n  0xec \e 1.0f ; 0xf4/0xf8 \e 0 ; 0xfc flags (0xc7 / 0xe7)\n  0x100/0x104/0x108 \e 1.0f scale ; 0x130 parent ; 0x138/0x140/0x158 child vector -> &node+0x160\n  0x148 -> &node+0x1a0 ; 0x1a0 entity back-ptr \e 0\n  0x1a8/0x1b0/0x1b8/0x1c0/0x1c8 \e -1  (so 0x1ac, the engine's FRAME_COUNT, is a -1-seeded int here)\n  0x1d0 \e 0 ; word 0x1d8 \e 0 ; 0x1e0/0x1e8/0x200 dirty-listener vector -> &node+0x208 ; 0x1f0 -> &node+0x210
func=jag::game::PlayerVarDomain::SetVarValue	func_addr=0x002eb000	kind=pre	insn_offset_from_entry=0	cu_index_from_entry=0	text=jag::game::PlayerVarDomain::setBit(this, varInfo, variant).\nSame shape as ::set but writes the SECOND value map at this+0x1c0c8, additionally stores -1 to\nthis+0x39608, and inserts into the dirty map with flag 0 (::set uses flag 1).\nEvidence: (1) the this+0x1c0c8 map is used by no other method and is the exact map Linux\n::setBit uses; (2) the scalar -1 write is present only in ::setBit on both platforms;\n(3) identical dirty-map insert but with the opposite flag value, matching the Linux pair.
